[{:db/id "q-99055/selection/54ac610d4774fee0ede6478b691a6eacc7c927529e978f51a18e757cb18525eb" :answer/id #uuid "7b258dc9-7cf4-5493-b19b-b722aba03996" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99055/selection/58b59430a6d8a203dee8eb1f5db9bf64be8a028e7f3877c3d0b22b4ec97f027d" :answer/id #uuid "348f57ba-3852-5576-b4f0-743e9143f6f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=\\sqrt{5}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99055/selection/565f087efc172cc689d6ac7a38ae1818e245a6f8995ecba68bcdf0ac5950b2ea" :answer/id #uuid "dffc52da-88e9-5e01-95fb-4d332eae9810" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99055/selection/63d45c0aa1ee41d1a647ec44aa152173aa975d3f3697b59fd5b3ba50982e0865" :answer/id #uuid "7b8c3db2-8d25-54f8-a8c7-c4e5285e96bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99055/selection/f7e369a59b250e2608c3a3b3880361c6a826317557d550b650731029b330e602" :answer/id #uuid "c80c3fe1-7442-50b1-b4a0-166e3c0b7c9c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99055/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "705be2b5-4a6c-5966-9ba6-952a23c3c3cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-99055/selection/f7e369a59b250e2608c3a3b3880361c6a826317557d550b650731029b330e602" :answer-field/choices ["q-99055/selection/54ac610d4774fee0ede6478b691a6eacc7c927529e978f51a18e757cb18525eb" "q-99055/selection/58b59430a6d8a203dee8eb1f5db9bf64be8a028e7f3877c3d0b22b4ec97f027d" "q-99055/selection/565f087efc172cc689d6ac7a38ae1818e245a6f8995ecba68bcdf0ac5950b2ea" "q-99055/selection/63d45c0aa1ee41d1a647ec44aa152173aa975d3f3697b59fd5b3ba50982e0865" "q-99055/selection/f7e369a59b250e2608c3a3b3880361c6a826317557d550b650731029b330e602"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99055" :question/id #uuid "63c773de-f51b-54ba-8cf5-768e0048eb88" :question/math-academy-id "q-99055" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-5)-(x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-5)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)-(x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5+6x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+5}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+5}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=5.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-99055/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99055"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127386/selection/a78fc75d11584ba017907466287da797a62ee22568671e12c5dac7e130287dc4" :answer/id #uuid "7f816a31-092d-5ba4-b0d7-ea39d7a68572" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.43" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127386/selection/65828953f30eb965b7d80317d1875adc4cb9ddec9441b0d443889f21caf96f2d" :answer/id #uuid "d18150f8-b957-54ba-82cc-6f9ad825b18b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.98" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127386/selection/f2a5add6335db383785cc7d7c4d0a3383293afb8aa32f7fa5bc36cd3b7a8ec36" :answer/id #uuid "b57c912e-e6be-5bfd-9bb7-860a0def19d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.26" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127386/selection/f719faf7aeabe73f194ea9a14f282fc33ef67e774f99f0ec74c1dcb4bb10c22b" :answer/id #uuid "be17fc96-5016-57ea-a7e8-457ed48b8060" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.55" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127386/selection/5f12c6ab41e1d9612e9ab112d6d95957796a8d7661edf5ad6726155700616cc5" :answer/id #uuid "dd541db5-66dc-504d-b758-616eb5c03e85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127386/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0cb29190-821e-54a5-82ae-f6e53cf766cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127386/selection/f2a5add6335db383785cc7d7c4d0a3383293afb8aa32f7fa5bc36cd3b7a8ec36" :answer-field/choices ["q-127386/selection/a78fc75d11584ba017907466287da797a62ee22568671e12c5dac7e130287dc4" "q-127386/selection/65828953f30eb965b7d80317d1875adc4cb9ddec9441b0d443889f21caf96f2d" "q-127386/selection/f2a5add6335db383785cc7d7c4d0a3383293afb8aa32f7fa5bc36cd3b7a8ec36" "q-127386/selection/f719faf7aeabe73f194ea9a14f282fc33ef67e774f99f0ec74c1dcb4bb10c22b" "q-127386/selection/5f12c6ab41e1d9612e9ab112d6d95957796a8d7661edf5ad6726155700616cc5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127386" :question/id #uuid "ba7d9686-4512-5ac3-be73-a640ac96e3b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-127386" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\  & =3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x} & =\\frac{9}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡3x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x≈-0.630\\,85$ to five decimal places\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x≈0.416\\,35$ to five decimal places\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ are \n\n$$\nx≈-0.630\\,85,\\,x≈0.416\\,35.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x≈-0.630\\,85$ and $x≈0.416\\,35.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ are ${x}_{1}≈-0.630\\,85$ and ${x}_{2}≈0.416\\,35.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-0.630\\,85\\cdot 0.416\\,35≈-0.26\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-127386/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127386"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344608/field-1/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer/id #uuid "0f3858d5-153b-5b2d-8787-d3569adb1d3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344608/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "c4849e24-9fa1-5c7f-9b87-7c7a6507f213" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344608/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "bfac7c8b-577b-501e-ad6e-be37867afb04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344608/field-1/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer-field/choices ["q-344608/field-1/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" "q-344608/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344608" :question/id #uuid "20f9ec3f-e21d-5b61-96bd-c285216e22be" :question/math-academy-id "q-344608" :question/problem "The critical point of the function $f(x)=(2x+1){e}^{x-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((2x+1){e}^{x-2}) \\\\  & ={(2x+1)}^{′}{e}^{x-2}+(2x+1){({e}^{x-2})}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{x-2}+(2x+1){e}^{x-2} \\\\  & =[2+(2x+1)]{e}^{x-2} \\\\  & =(2x+3){e}^{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+3){e}^{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x-2}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{x-2}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $f(x)$ is $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-344608/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344608"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344842/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "31ae11f7-59e6-5463-b97e-35e55e99e07b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344842/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b8383c5-17b3-59eb-bcd4-a81156ab263c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344842/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-344842/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344842/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "8c97e464-4f7b-5b14-a90c-3bcfc21eb2d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344842/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "13352d2e-9171-5908-9c56-2e91056859af" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344842/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-344842/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344842" :question/id #uuid "6387fc63-1d7e-52e2-8b83-73d6704377e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-344842" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{2}+12x)}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-15x+12){e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-15x+12){e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-15x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-5x+4) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-1)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are  $x=1$ and  $x=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344842/field-1" "q-344842/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344842"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345484/field-1/8c614710ad2e42ab410bb70d5b61a083c13e6919b705b640bdb0463542041a30" :answer/id #uuid "ac21cd45-3597-51a0-bd6b-82dc40ebe599" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345484/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e73b6396-71c3-5f96-9350-d61717a0c5bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-345484/field-1/8c614710ad2e42ab410bb70d5b61a083c13e6919b705b640bdb0463542041a30" :answer-field/choices ["q-345484/field-1/8c614710ad2e42ab410bb70d5b61a083c13e6919b705b640bdb0463542041a30"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345484/field-2/d569d64e1b94dabdc9f89c1070c848c6c96526a20f320d6b4971ae3a32604bcf" :answer/id #uuid "61edb762-254c-5e4e-80bb-81b769c1784f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345484/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "cbed6263-0731-57ec-a285-526382133e94" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-345484/field-2/d569d64e1b94dabdc9f89c1070c848c6c96526a20f320d6b4971ae3a32604bcf" :answer-field/choices ["q-345484/field-2/d569d64e1b94dabdc9f89c1070c848c6c96526a20f320d6b4971ae3a32604bcf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345484" :question/id #uuid "be26ab5d-93a6-5d26-b3a4-a77f406efe5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-345484" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **exact values** of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-345484/field-1" "q-345484/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345484"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
